Session 01a1cffb-4de1-75e6-b949-1e92d45a76cf (continuation). Plan: plan/2026-11-02a-push-pull-fanout-validation-fixes.md (+ .zh-tw.md), owner-supplied, committed unchanged as `41f4bfb7 [CEN-1637] docs: add push pull fan-out validation fixes plan`. Review baseline: 889901e9. Commit prefix [CEN-1637]. Toolchain: rust 1.85.0 pinned by rust-toolchain.toml; RocketMQ Java client 5.5.1.

Scope confirmation (Plan 02a sections 1-2): no production dispatch change was needed. `rg -n "PULL_RPC_WORKER_COUNT|PULL_RPC_QUEUE_CAPACITY" crates` returned no matches, so the fixed pool stays retired and the owned JoinSet dispatcher from Plan 02 is untouched. All work below is probe, checker, runner and scripted-peer validation.

Finding 1 - Java live probe lifecycle (`compat/java-reference/src/main/java/compat/LivePullFanoutProbe.java`).
Evidence: pinned RocketMQ client 5.5.1 `org.apache.rocketmq.client.impl.producer.DefaultMQProducerImpl#fetchPublishMessageQueues` (sources jar, line ~2037) returns an empty list unless serviceState == RUNNING; the previous probe called it before producer.start(), so a real run would fail with "no route for ...".
Decision: acquisition is now a seam (`acquireClients`) that starts the consumer first, then the producer, and registers each started client in a `StartedClients` owner whose `close()` runs LIFO on every failure path. Route lookup and sends happen only after both clients are RUNNING.
Added offline fixtures `LivePullFanoutProbeFixtures`, wired as Maven verify execution `verify-live-pull-fanout-fixtures`: pinned API rejects route lookup before producer start (1), acquisition cleanup closes started clients in reverse order (4), partial acquisition closes only the consumer (2).

Finding 2 - both recorders model completed requests, not active Pulls.
Decision: shared recorder shape in Java `Recorder` and Rust `FanoutProbeInner`, tracking `activePulls`/`active_pulls` keyed by queue id with the request opaque as identity, plus first-start map, earliest start, completed-response count, latched gate and a single violation slot.
Semantics implemented: (a) a second admission while one is outstanding records "queue N admitted a second Pull while opaque X was still outstanding" at that start and keeps the original outstanding entry; (b) an unmatched response records "a target Pull response for queue N / opaque X did not match an outstanding Pull"; (c) a target response before the barrier records the premature-response violation at the response itself, not on a later start; (d) the gate latches inside the same lock/critical section as the last admission (`attempt_latch` in both languages), so it cannot race with a response; (e) window expiry without a full barrier records "only K of 16 assigned queues had an outstanding Pull within the 5000 ms admission window"; (f) every listener callback emits a DELIVERY row, de-duplication only advances progress.
Rust unit tests in `integration-tests/src/bin/live_client.rs`: fanout_recorder_flags_a_response_that_lands_before_the_barrier, fanout_recorder_flags_a_second_admission_for_an_active_queue, fanout_recorder_latches_with_the_last_admission_and_allows_follow_ups, fanout_recorder_reports_a_barrier_the_window_cannot_reach, fanout_recorder_logs_every_delivery_callback. Mutation check: deleting the premature-response block made fanout_recorder_flags_a_response_that_lands_before_the_barrier fail; restoring it passed again.

Finding 3 - checker and runner evidence contract (`tests/differential/check_p10_fanout.py`, `tests/differential/run.sh`).
Checker now requires exactly one well-formed SHUTDOWN row, validates the GATE timestamp/span/distinct-queue count against recorded first PULL_START rows, rejects a gate that precedes any initial Pull or follows a target response, requires every SEND_START at/after the gate, requires each seeded send to have exactly one successful matching SEND_END (same queue, non-negative offset, end not before start), and rejects duplicate deliveries. Self-test grew from 9 to 20 synthetic cases: missing/duplicate/mismatched send end, pre-gate publish, negative offset, delivery after shutdown, duplicate delivery, malformed gate row.
Runner now owns resources and evidence: `create_fanout_group` records only groups whose `mqadmin updateSubGroup` succeeded (mutation test: deleting the record line fails the ownership self-test with "a partially created scenario must delete only the group it recorded"); `fanout_require_empty_topic` runs `mqadmin topicStatus` for both derived topics, records `$evidence_dir/topic-status.log`, and refuses any non-zero max offset before publishing; probe exit status, ready-file presence (`[[ -s ]]`) and checker verdict are all required before PASS is written. Function seams (`fanout_mqadmin_fn`, `fanout_topic_status_fn`, `fanout_java_probe_fn`, `fanout_rust_probe_fn`, `fanout_checker_fn`) let `selftest_fanout_evidence_contract` prove the contract without Docker: complete run records offsets and PASS; non-empty topic stops before any probe starts; gate timeout fails the scenario; a zero-exit probe with no ready file still fails; a failing checker overrides both probes.
Bash 3.2 note: empty-array expansion is guarded (`${fanout_groups[@]+"${fanout_groups[@]}"}`), and failure reasons are redirected with `cat "$tsv" "$stderr_file" >&2 2>/dev/null || true`; the reversed form hid operator output from the self-test assertions.

Finding 4 - scripted peer task ownership (`crates/rocketmq-classic-consumer/tests/push_pull_fanout.rs`).
Decision: `PeerTasks` records every accepted connection task (broker reader, writer, handler) with a label; each task carries a `LiveWitness` guard that decrements an `AtomicUsize` on completion or abort. Acceptance and the nameserver read loop now stop cooperatively through one broadcast shutdown channel instead of being aborted, and the broker reader selects on that channel so a client that never closes its socket cannot strand it. Teardown sends shutdown, joins the accept tasks and every recorded task under a 2s bound (`join_peer_task` panics on timeout or abnormal exit) and asserts the witness reached zero.
New test `peer_teardown_finishes_every_task_after_an_abandoned_connection` injects a client that connects and never speaks; mutation check: removing the reader cancellation made it fail with "peer task broker reader survived the 2s cleanup bound", proving the witness is load-bearing.

